    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the specific steps in the RAAS cascade? Like, what actually triggers the release of renin in the first place, and how does that whole chain reaction eventually lead to the production of Angiotensin II? I want to make sure I have the sequence down perfectly.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! The RAAS cascade starts when there is perceived low blood pressure, low sodium concentration, or sympathetic nervous system activation. This leads to the release of renin from the juxtaglomerular cells in the kidneys. Renin then converts angiotensinogen, a plasma protein produced by the liver, into angiotensin I. Angiotensin I is relatively inactive, but when it reaches the lungs, it encounters complementary proteins that convert it into the potent vasoconstrictor Angiotensin II. Angiotensin II has multiple effects: it constricts blood vessels to raise blood pressure, stimulates aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex to increase sodium and water retention, which also raises blood pressure, and promotes the release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the pituitary gland to further promote water retention. Lisinopril and other ACE inhibitors prevent the conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II, thereby inhibiting these actions and ultimately leading to lower blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
